-- The venue a sale happened at, and the index that makes a proximity audience
-- answerable.
--
-- Generated by hand and reviewed by hand, like `0013_sour_apocalypse.sql`,
-- `0014_branch_geolocation.sql`, `0015_consent_uniqueness.sql` and
-- `0016_push_subscriptions.sql`.
--
-- ## What this closes
--
-- `customer_events.branch_id` has existed since the event table was created,
-- and **nothing has ever written it**: the only insert in production is
-- `customer.profile_viewed`, which has no venue. So the question a proximity
-- campaign needs answered — "the customers who interacted at this branch" —
-- had no answer. The column was a shape without a writer.
--
-- This migration gives the interaction a venue to happen at:
--
-- - `checkins.branch_id` says where a sale happened. Nullable, because a sale
--   reported by a system that does not know its venue is still a sale.
-- - `idx_customer_events_branch` makes "the customers who interacted at this
--   venue, newest first" an index scan instead of a sequential scan per tenant.
--
-- ## Why the column is nullable and the FK is `SET NULL`
--
-- A sale outlives the venue it happened at. A venue that is deactivated or
-- removed takes the pointer with it, and the sale stays a fact about a place
-- that existed once. `ON DELETE SET NULL` is what makes that possible; the
-- alternative (`RESTRICT`, the default) would make a venue undeletable the
-- moment it had a single recorded sale.
--
-- ## Why the index is `(business_id, branch_id, created_at DESC)`
--
-- The query that reads it is always tenant-scoped, always filtered by venue,
-- and usually bounded by a recency window ("in the last N days"). The tenant
-- and the venue lead, and the timestamp rides third so the window is a range
-- on the same index instead of a filter after the fact.
--
-- ## What this migration does NOT do
--
-- **It does not store a customer's position.** The venue is a business's own
-- place, stated by the business. Where a customer is at any other moment is
-- still never recorded: the customer's location is not a datum this system
-- holds, and a campaign resolves an audience from interactions with a venue,
-- never from where a device happens to be.
--
-- **It does not enable row-level security.** No policy is created here; the
-- reviewed RLS rollout is that story.
--
-- ## Not applied here
--
-- `drizzle-kit migrate` against production is forbidden in this repository: the
-- ledger has drift and running the migrator would apply migrations twice. A
-- person applies this by hand, after review. This file is left on disk, unrun.
ALTER TABLE "checkins" ADD COLUMN "branch_id" uuid;--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "checkins" ADD CONSTRAINT "checkins_branch_id_branches_id_fk" FOREIGN KEY ("branch_id") REFERENCES "public"."branches"("id") ON DELETE set null ON UPDATE no action;--> statement-breakpoint
CREATE INDEX "idx_customer_events_branch" ON "customer_events" USING btree ("business_id","branch_id","created_at" DESC);
